<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yom Kippur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Day of Atonement, Yom HaKippurim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement, is the holiest day of the year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, held on the 10th of Tishrei, in late September or early October. Its main observances are a full fast and asceticism, with extended prayer services, usually in synagogue, and confessions of sin; with Rosh Hashanah it makes up the High Holy Days and closes the Ten Days of Repentance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> commands it in Leviticus and Numbers, and while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> stood the day had an elaborate sacrificial service, in which one of two goats was sent to Azazel, the origin of the term scapegoat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yom-kippur.jpg" alt="Yom Kippur" width="330" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurycy Gottlieb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurycy_Gottlieb_-_Jews_Praying_in_the_Synagogue_on_Yom_Kippur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> calls the day Yom HaKippurim, the day of the atonements, and forbids work on the tenth day of the seventh month, later called Tishrei. Its laws are given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> in three passages. Leviticus 16 allows Aaron to enter the sanctuary only through a complex sacrificial procedure to be performed yearly, while the people fast and do not work.</p><p>While the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> stood, the day was the occasion of an elaborate service. Lots were drawn on two goats, one offered as a sacrifice and the other sent to Azazel to be thrown off a cliff, the origin of the term scapegoat. The rabbis summarised the laws of the service in the Mishnah tractate Yoma. According to rabbinic tradition, the date on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> came down with the second set of tablets, after the sin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-calf">golden calf</a>, was Yom Kippur, and he announced that the people were forgiven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>According to Jewish tradition God inscribes each person's fate in the Book of Life on Rosh Hashanah, nine days earlier, and seals the verdict on Yom Kippur, the last of the Ten Days of Repentance. Repentance, teshuva, consists of regret, resolving not to repeat the sin, and confession before God. The prohibitions on work are those of Shabbat.</p><p>The day begins in the evening with Kol Nidre, chanted before the open ark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, except in the Italian and Romaniote rites that use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, which releases vows. Yom Kippur has five prayer services, one more than a Shabbat, ending with Ne'ila, and a confession is recited ten times during the day. In the Avodah of the Mussaf prayer, worshippers imagine themselves in the place of the priests of the Temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Yom Kippur is considered a day of Jewish unity: according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, a fast in which Jewish sinners do not take part is not valid. Reform synagogues generally have their largest attendance of the year on it and Rosh Hashanah, and many secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who keep no other holiday attend synagogue.</p><p>In Israel it is a legal holiday with no radio or television broadcasts, no public transport and closed airports, and eating in public, playing music or driving are almost universally avoided although not prohibited by law. Many Jewish athletes have declined to play on the day, most notably Sandy Koufax, who refused to start Game 1 of the 1965 World Series. Since 2016 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> has held no official meetings on Yom Kippur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yom Kippur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rosh Hashanah, the new year, and <b>Yom Kippur</b>, the Day of Atonement, a day of fasting and repentance, fall in the autumn; the pilgrimage festivals of Passover, which recalls the exodus from Egypt, Shavuot, which celebrates the giving of the Torah, and Sukkot, the festival of booths, recall…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Jewish tradition revered the Land of Israel, and prayers at the end of Passover and <b>Yom Kippur</b> close with “next year in Jerusalem”, but the return to Zion was held to depend on Divine Providence in a messianic age.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Hallel is not said on Rosh Hashanah and <b>Yom Kippur</b>, for the Talmud asks whether it is fitting to sing joyful praises while the King sits in judgement with the Books of Life and Death open before him, nor on Purim, for which the Talmud gives three reasons.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li><li><blockquote>…and was also used in Germany, Poland and Bohemia; a tune for the evenings of Rosh Hashana and <b>Yom Kippur</b> was probably the work of a Polish cantor of the early 17th century and spread westward; three tunes for the pilgrimage festivals go back at least to the…</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li></ul></section>
